winemac: a display with a camera housing is the part below it

A full-screen Space on a display with a camera housing ends below the housing: 1512x949 points
of 1512x982 on a 14-inch MacBook Pro. A game told the display is 1512x982 sizes its window to
that and keeps it so; macOS keeps the window at 1512x949. The bottom of the picture was cut
off, and every click landed 33 points below the pointer, because the window was not where
Windows thought it was.

When full-screen windows get a Space of their own (FINEWINE_FULLSCREEN=space) the display is
now, for Windows programs, the part below the housing:

 - it is reported at that height (3024x1898 pixels there), and so are its display modes of
   the current size;
 - Windows' screen coordinates start there. Quartz measures from the very top of the
   display, so every position that passes between the two (pointer events, moving the
   pointer, the desktop rectangle, the flip to Cocoa's coordinates) is moved by the height
   left out. The event calls are wrapped in cocoa_app.h so that no caller is missed;
 - a window counts as covering the display when it covers that part.

A window covering "the display" is then exactly the size of its Space: nothing is cut off or
rescaled, and positions agree. The rows left out only ever hold the menu bar. An earlier
attempt changed the reported height alone; windows were then placed 33 points too high and
clicks were off by as much. The other full-screen modes report the whole display.
FINEWINE_KEEP_CAMERA_ROWS=1 turns this off.

